<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tao Yuanming</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">365–427</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Tao Qian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tao Yuanming, also called Tao Qian, was a Chinese poet of the Eastern Jin and early Liu <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> periods. After brief service as an official he retired around 405 to farm in his home district near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lu">Mount Lu</a>, and his plain verse on rural life, wine and chrysanthemums made him the model of the recluse poet. His prose fable 'The Peach Blossom Spring' became one of the best-known works of Chinese literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tao-yuanming.jpg" alt="Tao Yuanming" width="320" height="334" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chen Hongshou · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ch%27en_Hung-shou_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tao Yuanming, also known as Tao Qian, had the courtesy name Yuanliang. He was born at Chaisang, now part of Jiujiang in Jiangxi Province, an area of great natural beauty near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lu">Mount Lu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-poyang">Poyang Lake</a>. His great-grandfather was the Jin general and governor Tao Kan, and his grandfather and father were officials who rose to county governor, but the family was moderately poor and had little political influence. His father died when he was eight. He lived under the Eastern Jin dynasty, whose rulers were marked by nepotism, corruption and violence, and which was replaced by the Liu Song in 419 or 420.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Tao spent more than ten years in government service, in five stints, in both civil and military posts, and made several trips down the Yangzi to the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>. He served under the usurpers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huan-xuan">Huan Xuan</a> and Liu Yu and under the weak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-an-of-jin">Emperor An of Jin</a>, and his poems from this time show him torn between ambition and a wish for solitude. In the spring of 405 he was an aide to a local commander. The death of his sister and his disgust at the corruption and infighting of the court prompted him to resign.</p><p>He said he would not bow like a servant for five pecks of grain, a saying that came into common use for swallowing one's pride for a meagre living. For the last 22 years of his life he lived in retirement on a small farm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>About 130 of his works survive, mostly poems and essays depicting a rural life of farming and drinking his homemade wine. His simple, direct style went against the literary norms of his time. His poems draw on Confucian, Buddhist and Taoist influences. He is also known for an unstrung guqin, which he said he valued without needing the sound of its strings. In his prose Peach Blossom Spring, a fisher gets lost, discovers a place cut off from the world and cannot find it again after he leaves. The name has become a standard Chinese term for a utopia.</p><p>Tao first gained fame locally as a hermit, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> he was revered as a poet's poet by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bai">Li Bai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/du-fu">Du Fu</a>. Zhong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rong-people">Rong</a> called him the father of recluse poetry. Su Shi of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> wrote poems answering Tao's in the same rhymes, and said that none of the other great poets achieved his stature. The Korean poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yi-hwang">Yi Hwang</a> was also inspired in part by him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tao Yuanming” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…calligraphy, was revered as the 'sage of calligraphy'; the painter Gu Kaizhi, to whom the Admonitions of the Instructress to the Court Ladies scroll is attributed, was among the first Chinese artists known by name; and <b>Tao Yuanming</b>'s poems of rural retreat became models for later Chinese poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>It deeply influenced Chinese poetry, from <b>Tao Yuanming</b> to Li Bai, landscape painting, with its vast mountains and tiny human figures, and calligraphy; the great calligrapher Wang Xizhi came from a family of Celestial Master followers.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lushan City, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=29.45,116.04&amp;year=405" aria-label="Lushan City on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7573 1087 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tao Yuanming"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8023 1357h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8023 1357h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=29.45,116.04&amp;year=405">What was here in 405 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Homewards I bend my steps. My fields, my gardens, are choked with weeds: should I not go? My soul has led a bondsman’s life: why should I remain to pine?&#8221;</p><footer>Homeward Bound (on leaving office), in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ah, how short a time it is that we are here! Why then not set our hearts at rest, ceasing to trouble whether we remain or go? What boots it to wear out the soul with anxious thoughts?&#8221;</p><footer>Homeward Bound (on leaving office), in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ere words be spoke, the heart is drunk; What need to call for wine?&#8221;</p><footer>On meeting an old friend in a far-off land (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Would you know how that is possible? A heart that is distant creates a wilderness round it.&#8221;</p><footer>Drinking wine, a poem &#183; tr. Arthur Waley, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42290/pg42290-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;White hair covers my temples, I am wrinkled and seared beyond repair, And though I have got five sons, They all hate paper and brush.&#8221;</p><footer>Blaming Sons &#183; tr. Arthur Waley, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42290/pg42290-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Long I lived checked by the bars of a cage: Now I have turned again to Nature and Freedom.&#8221;</p><footer>Returning to the Fields &#183; tr. Arthur Waley, 1918 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/42290/pg42290-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
